170	african_arguments	167	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/whilst-the-world-debates-somalilands-recognition-its-girls-remain-invisible/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=whilst-the-world-debates-somalilands-recogn	Whilst the World Debates Somaliland’s Recognition, Its Girls Remain Invisible <p>As international debate surrounding Somaliland&#8217;s recognition has returned to global headlines in recent months, questions of sovereignty and diplomacy have dominated the conversation. Reports of Israel&#8217;s recognition of Somaliland in December and Somaliland&#8217;s opening of an embassy in Jerusalem on 16 June have placed the territory&#8217;s political future firmly back in the international spotlight. Yet amid discussions of geopolitics and statehood, another story remains largely absent: almost every girl in Somaliland is expected to undergo female genital mutilation (FGM). “When a girl is born here, many people already know what will happen to her,” says Haboon Feysal, a student midwife [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/whilst-the-world-debates-somalilands-recognition-its-girls-remain-invisible/">Whilst the World Debates Somaliland&#8217;s Recognition, Its Girls Remain Invisible</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	en	\N	\N	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/whilst-the-world-debates-somalilands-recognition-its-girls-remain-invisible/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=whilst-the-world-debates-somalilands-recognition-its-girls-remain-invisible	2026-08-03 16:28:52+00	2026-08-06 12:54:05.079845+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK	neutral	0.072	[{"text": "Whilst", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "World Debates Somaliland", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Recognition", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Its Girls Remain Invisible", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Somaliland", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Reports", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Israel", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Jerusalem", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Yet", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "When", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Haboon Feysal", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "The", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "African Arguments", "label": "PERSON"}]	["somaliland", "recognition", "whilst", "debates", "girls", "remain", "invisible", "international", "girl", "debate"]	[]	t	2026-08-06 12:55:01.3483+00
172	african_arguments	169	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/48114/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=48114	Libya’s Elite Pact <p>In mid-June of 2026, the two rival factions claiming to be Libya’s official government agreed to a power sharing deal. In it, Prime Minister Abdul Dbeibah, the leader of the internationally recognised Government of National Unity (GNU) based in Triopli in western Libya, and Field Marshal Khalifa Haftar, the power behind of the unrecognised Government of National Stability (GNS) based in eastern Libya agreed to what is functionally a dual executive system for the new transitional government. Under the deal, Dbeibah would retain his current post as prime minister of the GNU, while Haftar would be given substantial sway over [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/48114/">Libya&#8217;s Elite Pact</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	en	\N	\N	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/48114/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=48114	2026-08-03 11:45:42+00	2026-08-06 12:54:05.102222+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK	neutral	-0.0023	[{"text": "Libya", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Elite Pact", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Prime Minister Abdul Dbeibah", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Government", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "National Unity", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Triopli", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Field Marshal Khalifa Haftar", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "National Stability", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Under", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Dbeibah", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Haftar", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "The", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "African Arguments", "label": "PERSON"}]	["libya", "elite", "pact", "agreed", "power", "deal", "prime", "minister", "dbeibah", "national"]	[]	t	2026-08-06 12:55:01.923158+00
174	african_arguments	171	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/after-accra-africas-health-sovereignty-needs-receipts/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=after-accra-africas-health-sovereignty-needs-receipts	After Accra, Africa’s Health Sovereignty Needs Receipts <p>Dr William Nii Ayitey Menson, Director of Health Financing for Africa at the ONE Campaign, argues that Africa’s health sovereignty will only become real when summit pledges translate into budget releases, functioning services and public accountability. At the African Union’s Extraordinary Summit on Health in Accra, I kept having one uncomfortable thought: I have heard this before. The words were true. Africa must finance more of its own health systems. Primary health care must be stronger. Medicines and vaccines must be manufactured closer to home. External financing is becoming less predictable. Families should not be ruined by hospital bills. Pooled [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/after-accra-africas-health-sovereignty-needs-receipts/">After Accra, Africa’s Health Sovereignty Needs Receipts</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	en	\N	\N	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/after-accra-africas-health-sovereignty-needs-receipts/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=after-accra-africas-health-sovereignty-needs-receipts	2026-08-03 08:56:40+00	2026-08-06 12:54:05.126424+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK	positive	0.1478	[{"text": "After Accra", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Health Sovereignty Needs Receipts", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Dr William Nii Ayitey Menson", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Health Financing", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Campaign", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "African Union", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Extraordinary Summit", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Health", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Accra", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "The", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Primary", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Medicines", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "External", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Families", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Pooled", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "African Arguments", "label": "PERSON"}]	["health", "africa", "accra", "sovereignty", "needs", "receipts", "financing", "summit", "african", "william"]	[]	t	2026-08-06 12:55:02.866218+00
175	african_arguments	172	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/when-a-story-of-homecoming-is-filmed-in-a-homeland-still-awaiting-return/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=when-a-story-of-homecoming-is-filmed-in-a-home	When a Story of Homecoming Is Filmed in a Homeland Still Awaiting Return <p>I am from Dakhla, on the Atlantic coast of Western Sahara. For thirteen years, I have been unable to return safely because of my human-rights advocacy, my defence of the Sahrawi people’s right to benefit from the resources of their land and to express their views freely. Friends and fellow Sahrawi activists have been prosecuted and imprisoned, while others have been pressured into silence. My close friend the Sahrawi activitst Hassana El Wali died in custody in a military hospital in Dakhla. Dozens of Sahrawis remain in prison, including members of the Gdeim Izik group as reported by the UN [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/when-a-story-of-homecoming-is-filmed-in-a-homeland-still-awaiting-return/">When a Story of Homecoming Is Filmed in a Homeland Still Awaiting Return</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	en	\N	\N	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/when-a-story-of-homecoming-is-filmed-in-a-homeland-still-awaiting-return/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=when-a-story-of-homecoming-is-filmed-in-a-homeland-still-awaiting-return	2026-08-02 22:22:26+00	2026-08-06 12:54:05.137986+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK	positive	0.1194	[{"text": "When", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Story", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Homecoming Is Filmed", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Homeland Still Awaiting Return", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Dakhla", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Atlantic", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Western Sahara", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "For", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Sahrawi", "label": "PERSON"}, {"text": "Friends", "label": "PERSON"}, {"