164	news	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/why-ethiopias-conflicts-keep-returning-the-question-ethiopias-national-dialogue-cannot-avoid/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=why-ethiopias-conflicts-ke	Why Ethiopia’s Conflicts Keep Returning: The Question Ethiopia’s National Dialogue Cannot Avoid <p>As Ethiopia’s National Dialogue seeks to chart a path toward peace, it faces an immense task. The Dialogue aims to address some of the country’s most difficult political, historical, institutional, social, and peacebuilding questions. Yet its success may ultimately depend on confronting a more basic question: why do Ethiopia’s major conflicts keep returning? A problem relocated, not resolved One answer lies in a problem that has received too little attention. The challenge of nation-building was not resolved after 1991; it was relocated. As political authority came to be organised through ethnoregional states, regional governments became central arenas for managing recognition, [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/why-ethiopias-conflicts-keep-returning-the-question-ethiopias-national-dialogue-cannot-avoid/">Why Ethiopia&#8217;s Conflicts Keep Returning: The Question Ethiopia&#8217;s National Dialogue Cannot Avoid</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/why-ethiopias-conflicts-keep-returning-the-question-ethiopias-national-dialogue-cannot-avoid/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=why-ethiopias-conflicts-keep-returning-the-question-ethiopias-national-dialogue-cannot-avoid	en	2026-08-06 08:24:45+00	2026-08-06 12:53:59.94372+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK
165	news	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/renegotiating-military-immunity-kenyas-new-terms-for-foreign-troops/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=renegotiating-military-immunity-kenyas-new-terms-fo	Renegotiating Military Immunity: Kenya’s New Terms for Foreign Troops <p>In July 2026, Britain suspended one of its flagship military exercises in Kenya after Nairobi withheld the licences required for troops to train in Laikipia. Five days later, the two governments reached an agreement allowing Exercise Haraka Storm to proceed. The immediate diplomatic crisis ended, but the deeper dispute did not; the standoff revealed that Kenya is no longer questioning the terms of Britain&#8217;s military presence but using licensing, parliamentary oversight, and domestic law to press for new legal terms governing how that presence operates. The strategy has been in development for several years, and it owes much to the [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/renegotiating-military-immunity-kenyas-new-terms-for-foreign-troops/">Renegotiating Military Immunity: Kenya&#8217;s New Terms for Foreign Troops</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/renegotiating-military-immunity-kenyas-new-terms-for-foreign-troops/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=renegotiating-military-immunity-kenyas-new-terms-for-foreign-troops	en	2026-08-06 08:05:24+00	2026-08-06 12:53:59.966875+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK
166	news	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/the-winter-circus-a-memoir/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=the-winter-circus-a-memoir	The Winter Circus: A Memoir <p>The Winter Circus: A Memoir (2026) by Leo Zeilig is published by the Uganda-based Editor House Facility (EHF). A genre-defying work blending memoir, fiction, and political reflection, it explores what it means to live through personal and global crisis. Moving between intimate experience and wider social forces, it captures a world of deep contradiction: extreme wealth alongside poverty, rising global movements alongside fragmentation. Set across Europe and Africa, it tells of a world on the cusp of radical change. The story takes place over a weekend in Paris. Four friends, Ivan, Louis, Patrice and Bhaskar travel to the French capital to [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/the-winter-circus-a-memoir/">The Winter Circus: A Memoir</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/the-winter-circus-a-memoir/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=the-winter-circus-a-memoir	en	2026-08-04 11:35:46+00	2026-08-06 12:53:59.98331+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK
167	news	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/whilst-the-world-debates-somalilands-recognition-its-girls-remain-invisible/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=whilst-the-world-debates-somalilands-recogn	Whilst the World Debates Somaliland’s Recognition, Its Girls Remain Invisible <p>As international debate surrounding Somaliland&#8217;s recognition has returned to global headlines in recent months, questions of sovereignty and diplomacy have dominated the conversation. Reports of Israel&#8217;s recognition of Somaliland in December and Somaliland&#8217;s opening of an embassy in Jerusalem on 16 June have placed the territory&#8217;s political future firmly back in the international spotlight. Yet amid discussions of geopolitics and statehood, another story remains largely absent: almost every girl in Somaliland is expected to undergo female genital mutilation (FGM). “When a girl is born here, many people already know what will happen to her,” says Haboon Feysal, a student midwife [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/whilst-the-world-debates-somalilands-recognition-its-girls-remain-invisible/">Whilst the World Debates Somaliland&#8217;s Recognition, Its Girls Remain Invisible</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/whilst-the-world-debates-somalilands-recognition-its-girls-remain-invisible/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=whilst-the-world-debates-somalilands-recognition-its-girls-remain-invisible	en	2026-08-03 16:28:52+00	2026-08-06 12:53:59.99977+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK
168	news	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/two-presidents-no-government-why-tigrays-war-is-coming-back/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=two-presidents-no-government-why-tigrays-war-is-coming-back	Two Presidents, No Government: Why Tigray’s War Is Coming Back <p>In Mekelle, the capital of Ethiopia&#8217;s northern Tigray region, two men call themselves president. One is Lieutenant General Tadesse Worede, appointed with Addis Ababa&#8217;s approval to run the region until elections could be held. The other is Debretsion Gebremichael, chairman of the party that governed Tigray for decades and led it through a war (2020-2022) that killed an estimated 600,000 people and displaced two to three million. On 5 May, a regional council that had not met in more than three years reunited and elected him. Addis Ababa treats that council as an illegal body: it was abolished under the [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/two-presidents-no-government-why-tigrays-war-is-coming-back/">Two Presidents, No Government: Why Tigray&#8217;s War Is Coming Back</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/two-presidents-no-government-why-tigrays-war-is-coming-back/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=two-presidents-no-government-why-tigrays-war-is-coming-back	en	2026-08-03 16:17:19+00	2026-08-06 12:54:00.033489+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK
