169	news	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/48114/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=48114	Libya’s Elite Pact <p>In mid-June of 2026, the two rival factions claiming to be Libya’s official government agreed to a power sharing deal. In it, Prime Minister Abdul Dbeibah, the leader of the internationally recognised Government of National Unity (GNU) based in Triopli in western Libya, and Field Marshal Khalifa Haftar, the power behind of the unrecognised Government of National Stability (GNS) based in eastern Libya agreed to what is functionally a dual executive system for the new transitional government. Under the deal, Dbeibah would retain his current post as prime minister of the GNU, while Haftar would be given substantial sway over [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/48114/">Libya&#8217;s Elite Pact</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/48114/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=48114	en	2026-08-03 11:45:42+00	2026-08-06 12:54:00.058818+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK
170	news	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/how-russia-lost-the-ability-to-leave-the-central-african-republic/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=how-russia-lost-the-ability-to-leave-the-central-afri	How Russia Lost the Ability to Leave the Central African Republic <p>When armed groups attacked the border post of Am-Dafok in northeastern CAR on 30 June 2026, most analysts asked the familiar question: who was behind the attack, and how resilient is Russia’s presence? The right question is different. Why can Russia not simply leave the Central African Republic — even if it wanted to? The answer changes how we understand the nature of external military presence in post-patronage Africa. Not Wagner, Not Africa Corps: A System. The debate over whether the transition from Wagner to Africa Corps is complete misses the point. The rebranding is real. The architecture did not [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/how-russia-lost-the-ability-to-leave-the-central-african-republic/">How Russia Lost the Ability to Leave the Central African Republic</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/how-russia-lost-the-ability-to-leave-the-central-african-republic/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=how-russia-lost-the-ability-to-leave-the-central-african-republic	en	2026-08-03 10:52:32+00	2026-08-06 12:54:00.088587+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK
171	news	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/after-accra-africas-health-sovereignty-needs-receipts/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=after-accra-africas-health-sovereignty-needs-receipts	After Accra, Africa’s Health Sovereignty Needs Receipts <p>Dr William Nii Ayitey Menson, Director of Health Financing for Africa at the ONE Campaign, argues that Africa’s health sovereignty will only become real when summit pledges translate into budget releases, functioning services and public accountability. At the African Union’s Extraordinary Summit on Health in Accra, I kept having one uncomfortable thought: I have heard this before. The words were true. Africa must finance more of its own health systems. Primary health care must be stronger. Medicines and vaccines must be manufactured closer to home. External financing is becoming less predictable. Families should not be ruined by hospital bills. Pooled [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/after-accra-africas-health-sovereignty-needs-receipts/">After Accra, Africa’s Health Sovereignty Needs Receipts</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/after-accra-africas-health-sovereignty-needs-receipts/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=after-accra-africas-health-sovereignty-needs-receipts	en	2026-08-03 08:56:40+00	2026-08-06 12:54:00.109957+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK
172	news	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/when-a-story-of-homecoming-is-filmed-in-a-homeland-still-awaiting-return/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=when-a-story-of-homecoming-is-filmed-in-a-home	When a Story of Homecoming Is Filmed in a Homeland Still Awaiting Return <p>I am from Dakhla, on the Atlantic coast of Western Sahara. For thirteen years, I have been unable to return safely because of my human-rights advocacy, my defence of the Sahrawi people’s right to benefit from the resources of their land and to express their views freely. Friends and fellow Sahrawi activists have been prosecuted and imprisoned, while others have been pressured into silence. My close friend the Sahrawi activitst Hassana El Wali died in custody in a military hospital in Dakhla. Dozens of Sahrawis remain in prison, including members of the Gdeim Izik group as reported by the UN [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/when-a-story-of-homecoming-is-filmed-in-a-homeland-still-awaiting-return/">When a Story of Homecoming Is Filmed in a Homeland Still Awaiting Return</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/when-a-story-of-homecoming-is-filmed-in-a-homeland-still-awaiting-return/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=when-a-story-of-homecoming-is-filmed-in-a-homeland-still-awaiting-return	en	2026-08-02 22:22:26+00	2026-08-06 12:54:00.134471+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK
173	news	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/cameroon-has-the-power-it-cannot-deliver-it/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=cameroon-has-the-power-it-cannot-deliver-it	Cameroon Has the Power. It Cannot Deliver It <p>The dams Cameroon dreamed of for fifty years are built and running. What blocks their megawatts is everything downstream: wires, transformers, unpaid bills, and the current that leaks away before it arrives. On the morning of 29 March 2025, half of Douala went quiet. Maintenance work had taken down the main transmission line into Cameroon&#8217;s economic capital. From six in the morning until six at night, nothing for industrial zones, food factories, a hospital&#8230; Upstream, all was well. The dams held their water. The turbines could turn. The electricity existed. It could not get through. Those twelve hours said what [&#8230;]</p>\n<p>The post <a href="https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/cameroon-has-the-power-it-cannot-deliver-it/">Cameroon Has the Power. It Cannot Deliver It</a> appeared first on <a href="https://africanarguments.org">African Arguments</a>.</p>	https://africanarguments.org/2026/08/cameroon-has-the-power-it-cannot-deliver-it/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=cameroon-has-the-power-it-cannot-deliver-it	en	2026-08-02 11:13:14+00	2026-08-06 12:54:00.154584+00	african_arguments:Nigeria diaspora community UK
